## Introduction
Support for hydrogen as a future source of clean energy has been rising over the last decade.[1] The dominant technology for $H_{2}$ production is through steam-reforming of natural gas. However, the purification of hydrogen from the common contaminants (e.g., $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , ${\sf N}_{2}$ , $C H_{4}$ and ${\sf H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ) is a requisite for the practical applications such as hydrogen fuel cells.[2] Membrane separation process has attracted significant interest due to its efficient energy consumption and smaller physical footprints compare with traditional separation technologies, such as distillation and liquidphase absorption. Polymeric membranes dominate the current membrane market for gas separation; however, their desirable performance is limited by the well-known trade-off between permeability and selectivity.[3] Furthermore, low ${\sf H}_{2}$ selectivity and short membrane lifetime due to rapid physical aging, and weak thermal and chemical stability are disadvantages of polymer membranes.[4] The concept of mixed matrix membranes has been extensively investigated to combine the advantage of high gas flux and processability of polymer membranes and the high selectivity of the microporous fillers.[5] However, the difficulty in precise control of the filler dispersion in the thin film membranes to achieve superior gas separation properties has limited their application.[6] Also, Zeolite membranes with high sieving ability such as SAPO-34 and DD3R have been recently considered for membrane application.[7] Despite the high selectivity of these materials, the gas permeance is low, and their chemical modification for optimizing the separation process is laborious.[8] Therefore, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been introduced for gas separation applications because of their great versatility, feasibility for pore size tuning, and facile functionalization.
MOFs generally display high gas flux while their $H_{2}/C O_{2}$ , $H_{2}/N_{2}$ and $H_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity are lower than the selectivity of typical zeolite membranes. For example, the ZIF-8 membrane showed $H_{2}/N_{2}$ and $H_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity of 11.6 and 13.0, respectively.[9] ZIF7 with a more rigid structure and smaller pore size $(\sim3\mathbb{A})$ than ZIF-8 exhibits slightly better separation performance, $H_{2}/N_{2}$ and $H_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity of 13.0 and 13.6, respectively.[10] Although there have been significant achievements in gas separation using MOF materials, it remains challenging to fabricate high selective gas membranes due to the larger pore size of the most MOF materials than the typical size of gas molecules.[11] Furthermore, physical and chemical modifications of MOF particles and microporous supports is vital to improve the adhesion of the MOF selective layer and prevent the formation of non-selective voids  [12]
Tuning of MOFs in terms of pore size and shape is a practical approach to increase the selectivity of membranes through the sieving effect. For example, Caro et al.[13] reported that a decrease in pore size of ZIF-8 to $3.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ could enhance the $H_{2}/C H_{4}$ and $H_{2}/N_{2}$ to 12 while the $H_{2}$ permeance was not significantly changed compared with the original ZIF-8.
In another work, a shorter ligand was applied to reduce the pore size of $[N i_{2}(L-a s p)_{2}$ (bipy)] MOF which led to high $H_{2}/C O_{2}$ and $H_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity of 39 and 26, respectively.[8] Covalent postmodification of ZIF-90 through the amination of free-aldehyde moieties in the MOF framework improves the $H_{2}/C H_{4}$ selectivity from 8 to 71. [15] The amine-functionalization constricted the pore size and reduced the numbers on non-selective voids between the MOF layers.
Generally, the crystalline nature of the MOFs limits the fabrication of well-intergrown and defect-free thin films or membranes.[16] Poor adhesion between the substrate and crystal growth in the seeding step is a common challenge for the development of MOF membranes. Different strategies to overcome this problem have been proposed, including altering the support surface pore size and chemistry,[17] functionalization,[18] and seeding.[19] Furthermore, modified synthesis protocols such as adding crystal growth modulators[20] or using microwave irradiation[21] have been applied. However, the most effective method is the chemical modification of the porous support to improve the nucleation and MOF growth on the substrate. The modification of alumina and titania supports by 3- aminopropyltriethoxysilane (APTES) as the covalent linker has extensively used.[18a, 22] In another study, the linkage between the seeds and alumina support was improved by the addition of polyethyleneimine into the aqueous seeding solution through the hydrogen bondings.[23] Chemical and thermal stability of membrane materials are crucial for practical applications. Unlike the majority of MOFs, zirconium-based MOFs are known for their high stability owing to strong coordination bonding between zirconium metal ion and the organic linker, making them promising for the development of membranes.[24] Among, Zirconium-based MOFs, UiO-66 is the best known for remarkable thermal and chemical stability, having $Z r_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}$ cluster connected by the terephthalic linker.[25] However, due to the relatively large pore size $(\sim0.6\ \mathsf{n m})$ , the selectivity of UiO-66 membrane follows the Knudsen selectivity values, i.e. H2/CO2\~5.[26]
Taking advantages of Zr-MOFs, we used UiO-66 as a base material to fabricate polycrystalline membranes for hydrogen separation applications. Particularly, we developed and characterized continuous UiO-66 membranes with a series of linkers varying in size and functionalities to study a variety of different desired separations (scheme 1). In addition, the molecular gas diffusion through the MOF membranes was simulated based on density functional theory (DFT) to predict the effect of the structure of ligands and MOFs pore aperture on the gas separation performance of the membranes. To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first report on the development and tuning of Zr-MOF membranes for hydrogen separation. Through a systematic change of the organic ligand chemistry, we are able to narrow down the pore apertures and significantly enhance the hydrogen separation properties of the MOF membranes. Generalization of the membrane fabrication method in Zr-MOFs as well as the validity of the effect of molecular design to enhance the molecular sieving ability of the membranes has a significant impact on paving the way for the advancement in this area.
## Results and Discussion
MOFs and membrane characterization. The synthesized ZrMOFs with terephthalic acid (H2BDC), 1,4- naphthalenedicarboxylic acid (H2NDC), 9,10- anthracenedicarboxylic acid (H2ADC), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $({\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{C}}-{\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{H}}_{2})$ ) and 2-bromoterephthalic acid (H2BDC-Br) as organic ligands are named as UiO-66, Naph, Anth, UiO-66-NH2 and UiO-66-Br, respectively. The prepared MOF particles have shown octahedral morphology, which is characteristic to UiO-66, with particle size ranged from 150 to $500~\mathsf{n m}$ depending on the type of organic ligand (Figure 1 a-c and Figure S1). Irregular and agglomerated structure of Naph MOF may have arisen from the highly asymmetric structure of $H_{2}N D C$ compared with other ligands. MOF particles have similar PXRD patterns like the one of UiO-66. Thus, the formation of Zr-MOFs and their crystal structures were proved to be identical to UiO-66 (Figure 1d). The FT-IR spectra of MOF particles showed peaks at the range of $1610{-}1550~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ and $1420{\cdot}1300\ \mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , which are attributed to carbonyl stretching of –COOH functional groups connecting $Z r_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}$ and ligands (Figure S2).
It is known that the chemical formula of UiO-66 with an ideal crystal structure is $Z_{\Gamma_{6}}\mathsf{O}_{4}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}(\mathsf{B D C})_{6}$ .[27] The presence of coordination modulators such as benzoic acid (BA) will build up defective MOFs with missing-ligands where modulator ligands replace the coordination deficiencies.
The incorporation of ligands and the modulator was confirmed by 1HNMR (Figures S3 and S4). 1HNMR spectra showed the inclusion of BA, in the MOF structures, which are coordinated or substituted with the organic ligands during the MOF formation. The chemical composition of UiO-66, Naph, and Anth were calculated by quantitative 1HNMR analysis (Table 1, Figures S5- S7 and NMR analysis in ESI).
TGA-DTA revealed that all of Zr-MOF particles are thermally stable in the air until around $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 1e and Figure S8).
Naph and Anth showed lower thermal degradation temperatures, compared with UiO-66. The calculated chemical formula of the MOFs indicates the higher ratio of missing ligands (about $25\%$ ) in Anth MOFs than the ones in UiO-66 or Naph (about $10\%$ ) (see Thermogravimetric analysis in ESI).  Therefore, the Anth structure contains more structural defects than the other two MOFs, which is in line with its lower thermal degradation temperature. The lower degradation temperature of Naph than UiO-66, with almost the same defect density, could be attributed to the lower temperature stability of the NDC ligand (Figure S9). Comparing the theoretical molecular weight values of the MOF particles and the experimental weight normalized to the residuals, $Z\mathsf{r O}_{2}$ , we calculated the chemical composition of the MOF samples (Table 1). The predicted chemical structures from the gravimetric analysis showed good agreement with the ones obtained from NMR characterization.
The bulkiness of organic ligands had a significant effect on the total amount of adsorbed ${\sf N}_{2}$ (at 77K), $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ (at 273K) and BET surface area (Figure 1f, h and Table S2) but the peak of micropore distribution remained constant at around $0.6\ \mathsf{n m}$ in all MOF structures (Figure 1g, i). This can be explained by the change in the number of pores accessible to the gas molecules. Although the crystal structure of MOF particles, and thus the internal microporosity, is unchanged, bulkier ligands would narrow down the aperture of pores preventing the adsorption of gas molecules. Hence, the utilization of bulkier ligands leads to a lessened adsorption capacity of the MOFs (lower uptake at low pressures) without significant change in the pore distribution peaks. Moreover, the pore width of MOF particles $(\sim0.6\ \mathsf{n m})$ is substantially larger than the kinetic diameter of gas molecules. However, the aperture of pores would be smaller. For example, it has been demonstrated that aperture of UiO-66 MOFs varies between 0.37 and $0.92\ \mathsf{n m}$ due to “gate opening and closing effects” derived from the rotation of benzene rings in ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ linker with a static width of $0.6\ \mathsf{n m}$ . For the bulkier ligands are used in this study, the expected value of the average static width must be smaller (see the molecular simulation in ESI).
The intact layer of MOF, with the thickness ranging from 300 nm to $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , were observed on the surface of the modified alumina substrates by cross-sectional FE-SEM (Figure 2a, c and Figure S11). The thickness of the MOF layers was almost twice that of the corresponding particle size since the membrane thickness is the product of particle size and the number of hydrothermal coating reactions. Furthermore, EDX elemental mapping showed the localization of the zirconium element at the layer seen on the substrates, indicating the formation of Zr-MOF layers (Figure 2b and Figure S12). The MOF membranes showed the crystallographic pattern of particles with the ones of the substrate, indicating the existence of MOF layers (Figure 2d).
The single gas permeances and ideal selectivities of each MOF membrane are summarized in Table S4 and S5. Figure 3 shows the single and mixed gas permeances of Zr-MOF membranes plotted as a function of kinetic diameters of penetrants. The UiO-66 membrane shows the peak of pore width at ${\sim}0.6\ \mathsf{n m}$ , which is much larger than the kinetic diameter of hydrogen molecules. Therefore, the resulting membrane has low selectivity towards hydrogen due to the lack of molecular sieving capability. Furthermore, the selectivity, for instance, $H_{2}/C O_{2}$ , was less than the theoretical Knudsen separation factor $(\mathsf{H}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}=4.7)$ However, when the bulkier organic ligands, H2NDC, and H2ADC, were used, the ideal selectivity was significantly enhanced to 15.3 and 31.9, respectively. This is due to the sieving effect derived from the constriction of pore apertures, which hinders larger gas molecules from passing through one cage to others, while hydrogen still can easily find the path to permeate across the membranes. On the other hand, the effect of functionality tagged ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ $(H_{2}B D C-N H_{2}$ and H2BDC-Br) was less significant on the separation performance of MOF membranes (Figure S14). Surprisingly, the ${\sf N}_{2}$ and $C H_{4}$ permeances were in the same or higher range compared with the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ though their kinetic diameters are larger than the one of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . Similar phenomena have also been reported in the graphene oxide membranes. [39] The strong adsorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules on the MOF pores surfaces limits its motion and decreases the permeance regardless of its relatively small kinetic diameter (Figure S15). Figure 3d shows $H_{2}/C O_{2}$ separation performance of the fabricated MOF membranes in contrast to the literature data and the corresponding Robeson upper bound. Although UiO-66 membrane is just beneath the upper bound, the overall performance has been generally improved upon the modification of MOFs. Notably, the introduction of bulkier ligands has a clear trend, where the hydrogen permeance decreases slightly while
Equimolar binary mixtures of ${\sf H}_{2}$ with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , N2, and CH4 were applied to investigate the effect of mixed gases on the separation performance of the MOF membranes (Figure S13).
Figure 3 shows the comparison of permeance and selectivity between single and mixed gas measurements. As a general trend hydrogen permeance decreased under mixed gas condition while the rest of gases almost maintained the single gas permeance values. The slower components with longer retention time in pores of MOFs delay in the passage of hydrogen molecules. The decrease in H2 permeance results in lower mixed gas H2 selectivity when it is compared to the ideal selectivity. However, the $H_{2}/C O_{2}$ selectivity of Naph and Anth samples was still in the higher plateau to exceed the Knudsen selectivity.
To further investigate the gas transport mechanism in MOF membranes, the solubility and diffusivity of the membranes were derived from the membrane’s permeability and gas sorption isotherms at room temperature (Figure S15). For the Zr-MOF particles, the gas solubility follows the order of $C O_{2}>C H_{4}>N_{2}>$ ${\sf H}_{2}$ , which is correlating well with the critical temperature of gas molecules. The high $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ solubility of $Z r$ -MOFs confirmed the existence of favorable intermolecular interactions between $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules and MOF structures. The values of gas solubility are comparable to those in microporous polymers (PIM-1) and porous organic cages.[40] The ${\sf H}_{2}$ molecules showed the largest diffusivity $(\sim10^{-6}\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}\ \mathsf{s}^{-1})$ in the $Z\mathsf{r}$ -MOFs among all other gases with the larger kinetic diameters. The high ${\sf H}_{2}/{\sf N}_{2}$ diffusivity selectivity of ZrMOF membranes, UiO-66 $(\sim31)$ , Naph $(\sim49)$ , and Anth $(\sim60)$ , verify the molecular sieving capability of the MOF layers.
Molecular simulation. In order to evaluate the effect of the ligand chemistry on the separation performance of MOF membranes, the gas diffusion mechanisms in $z_{r}$ -MOFs were investigated using  the nudged elastic band (NEB) method based on density functional theory (DFT) (Figure 4).[41]
Diffusion through MOFs or other microporous materials requires gas molecules to hop from one cage to an adjacent one by passing through the gates.[42] In UiO-66 and Anth, the gas molecules diffuse from the center of the tetrahedral cage (TetraC) and move through the center of the octahedral site (OctC) (Figure 4a and e). In Naph, the gas molecules begin from the center of the tetrahedral cage, and once the gas passes through Gate 1, the pathway is slightly bent towards the Gate 2 (Figure 4c). This adjustment is necessary since the gates in Naph are not aligned with the center of cages as in Anth and UiO-66.
The calculated energy profile of UiO-66 shows a minimum energy level at position 3 for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , where the gas molecules are stabilized by $\pi$ -interaction with the three surrounding benzenes (Figure 4b). The $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules have strong interactions with the aromatic bonds when they are located on the top of the benzene rings.[43] On the other hand, the highest barrier energy was achieved above the gate (location 5) due to the side-on interaction between the gas molecule and the benzene ring. Also, the gas molecule reaches maximum energy between positions 6 and 7 in Naph (Figure 4d).; the gas is mostly influenced by the side-on effect caused by the two center-pointing H2NDCs.The extended rings of H2ADC linker in Anth severely block the gates, and the gases are directly influenced by the distance to the benzene rings, where the closest point of contact is located in the center of the gate with the highest total energy. The $\pi$ interaction also stabilizes the gas molecule, which has a similar position as found within UiO-66 (Figure 4f).
Table 2 presents the diffusion energy barrier values and the differences between $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2.}$ With three additional benzenes covering the gate, Anth has the highest energy barriers among the other structures for both gases, therefore exhibits lower permeability. Anth also has the highest energy barrier difference $(\mathsf E_{\mathsf{a}}(\mathsf C\mathsf{O}_{2}){\mathsf{-E}}_{\mathsf{a}}(\mathsf H_{2}))$ compared with the other Zr-MOFs. Due to the larger kinetic diameter compared to ${\sf H}_{2}$ and an intense quadrupole moment, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ shows more substantial energy barrier diffusing in Anth, followed by Naph and then UiO-66. The degree of gate restriction by benzenes increases the energy barrier difference even though both gases have decreased permeability. The trend of energy barrier difference from our simulation results match the selectivity results in experiments, fully explaining the mechanism of gas diffusion in Zr MOFs.
## Conclusion
We have successfully fabricated Zr based MOF membranes on the modified alumina substrates using modulated synthesis protocol. The organic ligands are systematically changed to investigate the effect of the bulkiness or the functionality of ligands All the MOF particles have identical diffraction pattern in PXRD study, thus the same crystalline structure. Furthermore, MOF particles were proven to be stable in the air until $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ even when the ligands are changed from the primary terephthalic acid. The thin MOF layers made solvothermally on the substrates were found to keep the crystal structure and visualized by FE-SEM and EDX elemental mapping. The introduction of bulkier organic ligands, namely H2NDC and H2ADC, has significantly enhanced the selectivity towards hydrogen induced by molecular sieving effect. The molecular simulation results showed that the constricted pore apertures of these MOFs by the additional benzene ring(s), leads to the decrease in the diffusivity of larger penetrants while hydrogen was not remarkably affected. On the contrary, the effect of amino and bromo functionalities tagged to the benzene ring of organic linkers were not significant on the ${\sf H}_{2}$ separation properties. The developed method for fabrication of MOF membranes and the systematic investigation of the importance of organic linkers presented in this work are compelling to pave the way for the application of $z_{\r}$ -MOFs as membrane materials.
## Experimental Section
Reagent and Chemicals. Zirconium chloride (IV) (ZrCl4, Wako, $\geq95\quad\%)$ ), terephthalic acid (H2BDC, TCI, $\geq99\quad\%$ , 1,4- naphthalenedicarboxylic acid (H2NDC, TCI, $95\%$ ) 、 9,10- anthracenedicarboxylic acid (H2ADC, Sigma Aldrich, $\geq95~\%$ ), 2- aminoterephthalic acid (H2BDC-NH2, TCI, $\geq98$ $\%$ ), 2- bromoterephthalic acid (H2BDC-Br, TCI, $\geq98~\%$ )、benzoic acid (BA, TCI, $\geq99\$ ), anhydrous N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, Wako, $\geq99.5\%$ ), aluminum isopropoxide (Sigma Aldrich, $\geq99\ \%$ ), hydrochloric acid (HCl, Wako, $37\ \%$ )、polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, Polysciences, MW= 78,000 gmol-1), (3-aminopropyl) triethoxysilane (APTES, Sigma Aldrich, $\geq99\$ ), anhydrous toluene (Wako, $\geq99.5~\%$ ) were used without any purification. α- alumina disks with surface porosity of $100~\mathsf{n m}$ $25~\mathsf{m m}$ diameter) were purchased from Fraunhofer IKTS, Germany.
MOF synthesis. $Z r C l_{4}$ and organic ligands were dissolved into DMF in a vial, and then BA and water were added. The molar ratio of chemicals is presented in Table S1. After sonication to dissolve all the monomers, the solution was heated to $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathsf{h}$ . The precipitation was separated by centrifugation, subsequently washed by fresh DMF, methanol, and chloroform to remove unreacted materials, and then dried at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\ h$ under vacuum. The synthesized MOFs with ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ , ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{N D C}}$ , ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{A D C}}$ , H2BDC-NH2 and H2BDC-Br as organic ligands are named as UiO66, Naph, Anth, UiO-66-NH2 and UiO-66-Br, respectively.
Support modification. The $\mathtt{q}$ -alumina substrates were modified using a γ-alumina layer and surface functionalized by APTES. Boehmite (AlOOH) sol solution with PVA as an organic binder was prepared as reported by Gu et. al. [24b] The solution spun cast on α-alumina substrates at 2,000 rpm for $10{\mathsf{s}}$ . After drying at ambient temperature overnight, substrates are transferred to a hightemperature furnace and calcinated at $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\ h$ to obtain a porous $\mathsf{v}$ -alumina layer on the top surface. The grain size of the coated layer was less than $20~\mathsf{n m}$ as confirmed by SEM. The coated substrates were further treated by APTES in toluene at $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ according to the previously reported methods.[18b, 44]
MOF membrane fabrication. The MOF membranes were synthesized using a solvothermal process with the same composition as the MOF particles. The monomers were dissolved in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF under 30 min continuous stirring and sonication; the solution was then transferred to a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave $(100~\mathrm{mL})$ . The modified alumina substrates were fixed onto a homemade PTFE holder and were placed into the autoclave (see Figure S18). The autoclave was sealed and heated to $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathsf{h}$ . After naturally cooling down to the room temperature, the MOF membrane was removed carefully and washed with fresh DMF, methanol, and chloroform to remove unreacted materials. Finally, the membrane was activated at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\ h$ under vacuum. The synthesis, washing, and drying process were repeated twice to generate intergrown layers.
Computational analysis method. The NEB method was employed to investigate the change in the system total energy as a single molecule of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ or $H_{2}$ diffuses along the diffusion pathways (see computational study section and Figure S8 in ESI) in $Z r$ -MOFs. The energies were calculated using CASTEP[45] under the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) formalism with Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) density functional.[46] Ultrasoft pseudopotentials, energy cutoff of $520~{\tt e V}$ , and gamma point sampling of the $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{k}}$ -point space were used and all tasks were performed until the total energy change reached under $2\times10^{-6}$ eV/atom. The cell parameters taken from the experimental determination of UiO- $66^{[47]}$ and Naph[48] were not further optimized. A new model was built for Anth using the UiO-66 platform (see computational study section in ESI).
